<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><meta name="description" content="A sanitized home-server infrastructure case study."><title>Security · Home Server</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="../../styles.css"></head><body><header class="site-header"><a class="brand" href="../index.html" aria-label="Engineering Notebook home"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 4h6v6H4zM14 4h6v6h-6zM4 14h6v6H4zM14 14h6v6h-6z"/></svg><span class="nav-label">Engineering Notebook</span></a><nav aria-label="Home-server navigation"><div class="home-server-links"><a href="/home-server/">Overview</a><a href="/home-server/hardware/">Hardware</a><a class="active" href="/home-server/security/">Security</a><a href="/home-server/monitoring/">Monitoring</a><a href="/home-server/backups/">Backups</a><a href="/home-server/services/">Services</a><a href="/home-server/lessons/">Lessons</a></div><a href="/projects/"><span class="nav-label">← Projects</span></a></nav></header><main><section class="intro section-shell"><p class="eyebrow">HOME SERVER / SECURITY</p><h1>Security</h1><p class="lede">The server stays private by default. A portfolio page can explain the boundaries without becoming a map to the house.</p><button class="intro-scroll-cue" type="button" aria-label="Scroll to next section"></button></section><section class="section-shell"><div class="feature-card"><div class="card-kicker">PRIVATE BY DEFAULT</div><h3>The outside world gets a story, not an IP address.</h3><p>SSH is restricted to the trusted LAN and Tailscale access. UFW rules limit entry, services bind to localhost or private Docker networks, and the router has no port forwarding for Grafana, Prometheus, Docker, NUT, or SSH.</p><p>Grafana is reached from the Mac through an SSH tunnel. Public demonstration remains a separate decision that would require a reviewed relay, authentication, rate limits, and a sanitized read-only surface.</p></div></section><section class="section-shell"><div class="section-heading"><p class="eyebrow">REPEATABLE SETUP</p><h2>The commands are documented without publishing the keys.</h2><p class="section-intro">These examples show the workflow used during setup. The public version uses placeholders so it teaches the method without revealing access details.</p></div><div class="note-grid"><article class="note-card"><span>01 · CREATE</span><h3>Generate a key</h3><pre class="code-block"><code>ssh-keygen -t ed25519 \
  -f ~/.ssh/homeserver_ed25519</code></pre><p>The private key stays on the trusted workstation.</p></article><article class="note-card"><span>02 · INSTALL</span><h3>Install the public key</h3><pre class="code-block"><code>ssh-copy-id \
  USERNAME@SERVER_ADDRESS</code></pre><p>Only the public key is copied to the server.</p></article><article class="note-card"><span>03 · VERIFY</span><h3>Test the boundary</h3><pre class="code-block"><code>ssh -o PasswordAuthentication=no \
  USERNAME@SERVER_ADDRESS</code></pre><p>Key login succeeds before password login is disabled.</p></article></div></section><section class="section-shell"><div class="feature-card"><div class="card-kicker">PUBLIC-SAFE SCRIPTING</div><h3>Useful commands, no useful secrets.</h3><p>The examples intentionally use placeholders. Real private keys, usernames, addresses, hostnames, tokens, and recovery codes stay out of the repository and the portfolio.</p><div class="tag-row"><span>Placeholders only</span><span>No private keys</span><span>Key login verified</span><span>Password login disabled</span></div></div></section></main><script is:inline src="../../scroll-cue.js"></script></body></html>